cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "optcurves: consecutive convexity violations of gradient-descent optimization curves"
abstract: >-
  Performance-estimation (PEP) study, exact rational certificates and counterexamples, symbolic proofs and
  selected Lean-verified theorems on when the optimization curve of gradient descent with constant step size h/L
  can be concave at several consecutive steps, for L-smooth convex and strongly convex functions. For L-smooth
  convex functions, exact certificates and counterexamples together with a rescaling argument bracket the
  threshold h_n (the infimum of the step sizes with n consecutive violations) to within 1e-4 for n = 2 to 6, for
  example h_2 in [1.8634, 1.8635] and h_3 in [1.9, 1.9001]. For one violation, symbolic proofs checked in SymPy
  give, for every 0 < h < 2: the exact threshold for L-smooth mu-strongly convex functions with 0 < mu < L
  (kappa = L/mu), h_1(kappa) = (1 + kappa) - sqrt((1 + kappa)^2 - (7 kappa + 1)/2), no such function having a
  violation exactly when h <= h_1(kappa); and the exact worst case for 0 <= mu < L (for mu = 0, convex functions
  with a minimizer), v_1 <= L h^2 max(0, -q) / (2 (2 + h^2 (1 - m))) ||x_0 - x*||^2 with m = mu/L and
  q = 2 m h^2 - 4 (1 + m) h + m + 7, attained for every such h. The Lean theorems cover three exact certificates
  (no two consecutive violations at h = 9/5 and h = 9317/5000, no three at h = 19/10) and take the interpolation
  inequalities of smooth convex functions as hypotheses. The PEP thresholds for two or more consecutive
  violations of strongly convex functions are numerical.
